    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125643,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 113,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Syncytial myoepithelioma; myoepithelial carcinoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:55:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125644,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 113,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cutaneous myoepithelioma shows a wide anatomical distribution, with a predilection for the extremities {12753168; 14745720; 23588365}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:55:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125645,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 113,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients with cutaneous myoepithelioma present with a painless, slow-growing papule or nodule {12753168; 14745720; 23588365; 33340147}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:55:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125646,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 113,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Myoepithelioma usually arises in adults, with a peak incidence in the third through fifth decades and an M:F&nbsp;ratio of 3:1 {10389622; 11224605; 14745720; 23588365}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:55:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125647,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 113,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:55:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125648,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 113,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>More than 80% of syncytial myoepitheliomas harbour an <em>EWSR1</em>::<em>PBX3</em> fusion {23588365; 30834570; 31135487; 34854109}. <em>EWSR1</em> rearrangements are less common in other cutaneous myoepitheliomas {21725291}. Cutaneous mixed tumours harbour <em>PLAG1</em> rearrangements, as do a small subset of myoepitheliomas lacking ductal differentiation {22038920; 23630011}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:55:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125649,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 113,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cutaneous myoepitheliomas are well circumscribed and range from 3 to 27&nbsp;mm (mean: 10&nbsp;mm) {14745720; 23588365}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:55:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125650,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 113,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cutaneous myoepitheliomas are well circumscribed, occasionally with an epidermal collarette. Some tumours are lobulated and composed of a trabecular or nested arrangement of epithelioid, spindled, plasmacytoid, or clear cells, usually within a myxoid or hyalinized stroma, with areas of solid, sheet-like growth {12753168; 14745720}. Extension into the subcutis may be seen. Cutaneous syncytial myoepitheliomas (accounting for 50% of cases) are composed of sheets of histiocytoid to short spindle cells with pale eosinophilic cytoplasm; one&nbsp;third show adipocytic metaplasia, whereas chondroid differentiation is rare {23588365}. Myoepithelial carcinomas are exceptionally rare and often show marked nuclear atypia and a high mitotic rate; the minimal criteria for malignancy have not been established in the skin {12753168; 14745720; 23992447}. Cutaneous myoepitheliomas are consistently positive for EMA and S100. Expression of keratins, GFAP, SOX10, p63, and SMA is variable {10389622; 12753168; 14745720}. Syncytial myoepitheliomas are negative for keratins {23588365}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:55:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 775,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125651,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 113,
        "headingId": 775,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:55:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 833,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 776,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125652,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 113,
        "headingId": 776,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Rearrangements of <em>EWSR1</em> can be detected.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:55:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 836,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 779,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125653,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 113,
        "headingId": 779,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> lobulated, trabecular, or nested architecture with variably epithelioid, spindled, plasmacytoid, or clear cells within a myxoid or hyalinized stroma; for syncytial myoepithelioma: sheets of histiocytoid or short spindle cells with pale eosinophilic cytoplasm; EMA and S100 positivity.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> positivity for EMA/keratins and for S100, SOX10, or GFAP.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:55:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 834,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 777,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125654,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 113,
        "headingId": 777,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:55:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 835,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 778,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125655,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 113,
        "headingId": 778,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cutaneous myoepitheliomas are benign and rarely recur {10389622; 14745720; 23588365}. Myoepithelial carcinomas may metastasize and follow an aggressive course {23992447}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:55:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]